EXT. SALT FLAT - DAY

White to the horizon. Heat shimmer. A burnt-out van tilts in the distance like a toy.

NAOMI, 30s, sunburned, drags a jerry can by its handle. Her canteen is empty. She stops, swaying.

NAOMI
(to herself)
Keep walking. Keep walking.

A figure on the horizon. Naomi shades her eyes. A WOMAN, 40s, in a ragged parka, wobbles closer. She carries a water jug. She sees Naomi and stops.

WOMAN
(hoarse)
Is that water?

NAOMI
Gas.

WOMAN
Damn.

Naomi sets down the can. The woman does the same with her jug. They stare across ten feet of white.

NAOMI
Where'd you come from?

WOMAN
East. A bus. I'm told there was a bus.

NAOMI
There was. It left.

The woman sits in the salt. Naomi sits too. Neither speaks. The wind scrapes the flat.

NAOMI (CONT'D)
(to the sky)
Anybody want to explain this?

WOMAN
(to Naomi)
What's your name?

NAOMI
Naomi.

WOMAN
Elspeth. My friends call me Elsie.

ELSPETH nudges the jug across the salt.

ELSPETH (CONT'D)
Half each. You carry the gas. I carry the rest.

Naomi takes the jug. She drinks, passes it back, and nods.

NAOMI
She'd have hated this, my mother. Heat.

ELSPETH
Mine too.

They stand, shoulder to shoulder, and start toward the van.
